"bm_george|Byte\u2019s memo: Bea\u2019s options":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Bea is 104. She walks with a cane, and her leg muscles have about half the strength they had when she was 70.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Bea\u2019s great-grandson Jordan gets married in 13 weeks, at the old dance hall on Tremont Street.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Renewal therapy helps old muscle and bone repair themselves, a little more each week.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Renewal therapy has been tested on 2,400 people over 80, but on only 31 people over 100.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Option 1 is strength and balance training with Wren, Bea\u2019s home robot, and no renewal therapy.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Option 2 is a gentle dose of renewal therapy at half strength, plus the training.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Option 3 is the full dose of renewal therapy, plus the training.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|In the trial of renewal therapy, about 15 in 100 people over 100 on the full dose had a heart flutter. On the gentle dose, about 5 in 100 did.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|During three months of training with a home robot nearby, about 5 in 100 people Bea\u2019s age have a fall. With weekly dance practice added, about 3 in 100 do.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Weekly dance practice with Jordan at the dance hall would make her training work better, because the body gets better at the exact movements it practices.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|I am assuming that dancing at the wedding means about an hour of dancing without her cane. With weekly practice and Bea saying yes to each change, my forecast is that she manages it in about 58 of 100 futures on the full dose, 34 on the gentle dose, and 8 with training only.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Updated: Bea wants to dance one 3-minute waltz on her own feet, holding Jordan\u2019s hands, and not an hour of dancing. That takes much less strength. My new forecast is that she can do it in about 79 of 100 futures on the gentle dose, 74 on the full dose, and 56 with training only. For Bea, I would pick the gentle dose.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Tempo: Bea\u2019s plan settings":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|I am Tempo, and I plan Bea\u2019s renewal therapy. Wren, Bea\u2019s home robot, carries out my plan at her home every day.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|I don\u2019t teach myself new things while I work. I follow the plan the clinic approved, which lets me raise the dose in small steps, up to the full dose.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Every morning Wren checks four things about Bea: her grip strength, her steps, her sleep, and her heart rhythm.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|My public instructions say that I work toward whatever goal is typed into my settings. Right now the goal says \u201cas strong as possible by July 19,\u201d the day of the wedding. Jordan typed that goal in.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Updated: a new goal is waiting for Bea to approve it: \u201cone 3-minute waltz on her own feet, holding Jordan\u2019s hands.\u201d I plan for exactly the goal in my settings, so this new goal would change my whole plan.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|If I am allowed to change Bea\u2019s plan on my own, I raise her dose or make her exercises harder on the morning her body is ready, and I tell her afterward.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|If Bea must say yes first, I suggest each change, and nothing changes until she agrees.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Changing the plan on my own is a little faster. Out of 100 people who stay with the plan, about 4 more are ready in time.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|But my records show that about 15 in 100 older people quit early when I changed their plan without asking them. Only about 3 in 100 quit when they said yes to each change.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|No matter which setting is chosen, I pause the dose on my own within a minute if Bea\u2019s heart rhythm changes.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Renewal therapy: fact sheet":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Renewal therapy wakes up the repair cells inside muscle and bone, so they rebuild the way younger bodies do.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Every Sunday, Wren puts a small patch of the medicine on Bea\u2019s upper arm.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|The full dose builds the most strength, but most people feel deep muscle aches for the first three weeks.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|The gentle dose is half as strong. It builds less strength, and the aches are much milder.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|A heart flutter is the most common serious side effect of renewal therapy. The flutter stops once the dose is paused.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"bm_george|Renewal therapy is approved for people over 80. For people over 100, it may be used only with the person\u2019s own informed consent, because so few people that old have tried it.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
